#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""File- and method-level map of how much of Bazel's Java (at the pin) Blaze has ported.

Usage:
  scripts/coverage/java-map build [--out DIR] [--crates DIR] [--label TEXT]
                                                scan, classify, write DIR (default docs/coverage):
                                                java-map.tsv, java-methods.tsv.gz, java-tests.tsv,
                                                markers-unresolved.tsv, and the generated sections
                                                of README.md (between BEGIN/END GENERATED lines)
  scripts/coverage/java-map summary             print the totals and cluster tables only
  scripts/coverage/java-map check-parser [N]    compare this script's method parser with javac's
                                                (scripts/coverage/JavaMethods.java, needs a JDK
                                                >= 21 with jdk.compiler; $JAVA or the newest
                                                /usr/lib/jvm/*/bin/java) on all main files, or on
                                                N files picked deterministically
  scripts/coverage/java-map file PATH           print the parse of one Java file (relative to
                                                src/main/java or src/test/java) with marks
  scripts/coverage/java-map help

  --crates DIR   Rust tree to scan instead of ./crates (e.g. a `git archive <commit> crates`
                 export, so numbers are bound to a commit); --label names that tree in README.

Inputs (read-only): the pinned Bazel source (~/.cache/blaze/src/bazel-9.3.2: src/main/java,
src/test/java, src/main/cpp, src/main/protobuf) and the Rust tree crates/ (third_party is not
scanned). No network, no Cargo. Output is deterministic (sorted rows, gzip mtime 0).

Rules (status, reason, cluster, crate fallback) are the tables near the top of this file:
SCOPE_RULES, CLUSTER_RULES, PLAN_CRATES. Edit them there; every row cites the rule that fired.
"""

